<TopicPage slug="subjects/chemistry/intermediate/chemical-equilibrium">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Most reactions run both ways, and they <strong>settle into a truce, not a finish
    line</strong>. The forward reaction makes products; the reverse reaction consumes them;
    when the two rates equal, concentrations freeze — <em>dynamic equilibrium</em>. Frozen
    does not mean finished, and it does not mean equal: the balance point is a property of
    the reaction itself (its equilibrium constant) plus whatever you do to the system next.
    Master this and you can predict the yields of industry, the pH of your blood and the
    behaviour of the oceans — all the same principle wearing different clothes, and one of
    the most load-bearing ideas in the entire subject.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="reactants and products converging — where the curves cross is the equilibrium mixture" widget="plotter" config={eqCfg} />

  <p>
    The curves swap slopes and settle at fixed levels: reaction still roaring in both
    directions, but with no net change. That's why Haber-process ammonia factories can't push
    reactions to completion — and why they instead recycle unreacted gas endlessly.
  </p>

  <h2>Physical change, chemical change — and the reversible middle</h2>
  <p>
    The syllabus's opening distinction sets the stage. A <strong>physical change</strong>
    alters arrangement, not substance: ice to water, salt dissolving, sugar melting — usually
    reversed by undoing the condition. A <strong>chemical change</strong> makes new
    substances with new bonds — burning magnesium, rusting iron — and "usually can't be
    reversed by cooling" is the classroom shorthand. But the honest picture is a spectrum:
    many chemical changes <em>are</em> reversible, and those are this chapter's subject. The
    classic demonstration: blue hydrated copper sulfate, CuSO₄·5H₂O, releases its water when
    heated and turns white (anhydrous CuSO₄);
    add water and the blue returns, with the test-tube warm because rehydration releases the
    same energy back. Forward and reverse, on command. Ammonium chloride does it more
    dramatically: heated, it decomposes to ammonia and hydrogen chloride gases; cooled, the
    gases recombine on the cold glass — a reaction that runs "up" the test tube and "down"
    it at the same time.
  </p>

  <h2>Dynamic equilibrium: busy and unchanged</h2>
  <p>
    Two conditions define equilibrium. First, a <strong>closed system</strong> — nothing
    escapes (that's why the ammonium chloride tube is sealed and why open reactions never
    reach equilibrium: products leave). Second, the <strong>forward and reverse rates are
    equal</strong>, so concentrations are constant. The word <em>dynamic</em> is the whole
    point: at equilibrium, molecules are still colliding and reacting billions of times per
    second — every forward event is balanced by a reverse one, like a department store
    where customers enter exactly as fast as they leave. Evidence that the traffic
    continues: radioactive tracer atoms swap between reactants and products at equilibrium
    even though concentrations never budge. It is motion in balance, not stillness — the
    busiest stillness in science.
  </p>
  <p>
    A second demonstration makes temperature's effect visible in real time. Sealed-tube
    nitrogen dioxide exists in equilibrium with its colourless dimer: 2NO₂ (choking brown
    gas) ⇌ N₂O₄ (colourless), with dimerisation exothermic. Stand the tube in hot water and
    it darkens (heat pushes the endothermic direction — more NO₂); ice water and it pales.
    No measurement kit needed: the equilibrium position is the colour. Cobalt complexes do
    the same trick in solution (pink when cool, blue when hot — the chemistry inside
    novelty mood mugs), and both demos make the point that "position of equilibrium" is a
    physical, movable thing, not a label.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> equilibrium means "half and half", or that the amounts
  of reactants and products are equal. What equalises is the <em>rates</em>, not the
  <em>amounts</em> — the settled mixture can be 99% products (strong acids and bases
  neutralising), 99% reactants, or anywhere between, depending on the reaction's equilibrium
  constant. The store analogy holds: a store can be at capacity with 1 customer inside or
  1,000.</p>

  <h2>The equilibrium constant: how far a reaction goes</h2>
  <p>
    For a reversible reaction at equilibrium, the ratio of product concentrations to reactant
    concentrations (each raised to its coefficient) is a fixed number at a given
    temperature — the <strong>equilibrium constant, K</strong>. For the Haber reaction,
    N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃, the expression is K = [NH₃]²/([N₂]·[H₂]³). The magnitude tells you the
    destination at a glance: K far above 1 means the equilibrium lies to the products
    (reaction "goes"); K far below 1 means it barely starts. Two rules about K repay
    memorising. It changes <strong>only with temperature</strong> — concentration, pressure
    and catalysts all leave it untouched (they move the system's position through the same
    K, not K itself). And it is the ratio of the two rate constants, K = k_forward/k_reverse
    — the kinetics chapter's machinery and the equilibrium chapter's are the same engine
    seen from two sides. Estimating from the widget's curves: the settling mixture ends
    around 0.33 units of product against 0.07 of reactant — a K near 5, a reaction that
    "mostly goes".
  </p>

  <h2>Le Chatelier's principle: poke the balance</h2>
  <p>
    Henri Le Chatelier's 1884 principle is qualitative but powerful: <strong>stress a
    system at equilibrium and it shifts to oppose the stress</strong>.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Add a reagent → the system consumes it.</strong> Your blood is a buffered
    equilibrium: CO₂ + H₂O ⇌ H₂CO₃ ⇌ H⁺ + HCO₃⁻. Breathe fast (hyperventilate) and you dump
    CO₂, the equilibrium pulls left, H⁺ falls, and blood pH rises enough to constrict
    blood vessels to your brain — the dizziness and tingling of a panic attack, treated by
    breathing into a paper bag (re-inhaling CO₂). Every breathing rule in first aid is this
    bullet.</li>
    <li><strong>Squeeze (raise pressure) → the side with fewer gas molecules wins.</strong>
    N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃ goes from 4 gaseous molecules to 2 — high pressure favours ammonia.
    Conversely, more molecules on the product side means pressure favours reactants, and
    equal molecule counts means pressure does nothing (a favourite trick question).</li>
    <li><strong>Heat helps the endothermic direction.</strong> Treat heat as a reagent: if
    the forward reaction releases heat (exothermic), heating pushes the balance
    <em>backwards</em>, and the equilibrium constant K itself falls. This is why
    exothermic syntheses are cooled and endothermic ones (limestone decomposition)
    run hot.</li>
    <li><strong>Catalysts change speed, never position.</strong> They accelerate forward
    and reverse equally, so equilibrium arrives sooner and looks exactly the same — the
    most-misquoted fact in the subject, and the reason industrial chemists say "catalysts
    buy time, not yield".</li>
  </ul>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "raising the temperature always increases the yield."
  Only for endothermic reactions. For exothermic ones — ammonia synthesis, the contact
  process — heat is effectively a product, and higher temperature drives the yield
  <em>down</em> while speeding the reaction up. The tension between those two effects is the
  entire plot of industrial chemistry.</p>

  <h2>Three drills, worked</h2>
  <p>
    Predict-then-check is how the skill is built. <strong>Drill 1:</strong> the ester
    equilibrium ethanoic acid + ethanol ⇌ ethyl ethanoate + water. Add more ethanol: Q falls
    below K, so the system runs forward, converting the added ethanol (and more acid) into
    ester — the perfumery trick of driving esterification with excess alcohol.
    <strong>Drill 2:</strong> the same reaction in a sealed flask at constant volume — you
    compress it. Count gaseous molecules: 2 on each side. Equal counts means compression
    changes Q not at all: no shift (Le Chatelier's silent case). <strong>Drill 3:</strong>
    hydrating copper sulfate, CuSO₄ (white) + 5H₂O ⇌ CuSO₄·5H₂O (blue), exothermic forward.
    In humid air the equilibrium runs forward (the white crystals slowly turn blue — a
    homemade humidity indicator); heat them and water is expelled. Each drill is the same
    logic — compare Q to K, count molecules, track heat — at the level the exam expects and
    the industry practises.
  </p>

  <h2>Case study: the Haber process, the compromise incarnate</h2>
  <p>
    Ammonia feeds the world — roughly half the nitrogen in your body passed through the
    Haber–Bosch process, which still consumes around 1–2% of humanity's energy. Its
    chemistry is one line: N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃, exothermic (ΔH = −92 kJ/mol), with fewer gas
    molecules on the right. Le Chatelier then dictates the dream conditions: <em>cold</em>
    (for yield) and <em>high pressure</em> (for yield). Reality disagrees: below about
    300 °C the reaction is uselessly slow (nitrogen's triple bond barely cracks), and
    pressures much beyond 200 atmospheres cost more in steel and pumping than the extra
    yield earns. Industry's answer is the compromise: <strong>about 450 °C, 200 atm, an
    iron catalyst</strong> — conditions where maybe 15% of each pass converts. The
    equilibrium never being reached-or-favourable is then defeated by engineering instead:
    ammonia is condensed out of the loop (removing product shifts the balance forward, per
    Le Chatelier), and unreacted N₂/H₂ recirculates. Overall conversion approaches 97%.
    The lesson generalises: equilibrium chemistry sets the destinations; kinetics and
    economics choose the road.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>K, Q and the direction of travel.</strong> At any instant you can compute the
    reaction quotient Q (same expression as K, any concentrations). Q below K → net forward
    reaction; Q above K → net reverse; Q = K → equilibrium. This turns "which way will it
    go?" into arithmetic — the standard tool for predicting a shift quantitatively, and the
    gateway to ICE tables (Initial, Change, Equilibrium) in the advanced tier. The
    temperature dependence of K is dramatic enough to quantify: for the Haber equilibrium,
    K is astronomically large at 25 °C (thermodynamically, ammonia "should" form almost
    completely at room temperature) yet modest at 450 °C — the numbers behind the
    compromise, and a clean demonstration that thermodynamics decides the destination
    while kinetics decides whether you live to see it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Equilibrium in water — the chemistry of life's solvent.</strong> Water itself is
    an equilibrium: 2H₂O ⇌ H₃O⁺ + OH⁻, with K (the ion product) = 10⁻¹⁴ at 25 °C — the
    number behind the entire pH scale (the acids-and-bases chapters build on it directly).
    The ocean is a three-phase carbonate equilibrium (CO₂ gas ⇌ dissolved CO₂ ⇌
    HCO₃⁻ ⇌ CO₃²⁻) that buffers Earth's climate chemistry: add atmospheric CO₂ and the
    seawater equilibrium shifts toward more dissolved carbon and more H⁺ — ocean
    acidification is Le Chatelier applied to the planet. Blood, seawater, buffering
    solutions: one principle, cascading.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>When Le Chatelier fails.</strong> The principle is a heuristic, not a law, and
    it misleads in edge cases — notably when "adding" a reagent changes volume, or in
    systems with competing equilibria where the intuitive shift is overwhelmed by a
    stronger effect. Thermodynamics gives the rigorous answer: equilibrium position follows
    from the Gibbs free energy (the enthalpy chapter's next step), and every Le Chatelier
    prediction is a consequence, not a cause. The advanced tier replaces the principle with
    ΔG = ΔH − TΔS; until then, use the principle but respect its range.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>One principle, many industries.</strong> The contact process (sulfuric acid,
    2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃, exothermic, fewer molecules) is Haber's sibling — same compromise
    (~450 °C, modest pressure, V₂O₅ catalyst), same recycle logic. Methanol synthesis,
    ethene hydration, and the water-gas shift that purifies hydrogen all run the same
    design pattern. Chemical equilibrium is the rare topic where the exam question and the
    £100-million plant are the same question.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Le Chatelier (1884); Haber–Bosch process histories (Smil, Enriching the Earth); OpenStax, Chemistry 2e (ch. 13, Fundamental Equilibrium Concepts); IPCC ocean carbonate chemistry summaries.</p>
</TopicPage>
